sections.body:
`if` runs the code inside the curly braces when the condition in the parentheses is true. `else` runs when the condition is false:

```js
const fuel = 15;
if (fuel < 20) {
  console.log("Warning: fuel is low!");
} else {
  console.log("Fuel is fine.");
}
```

The lines inside the curly braces are indented by two spaces so they're easy to read.

Conditions are tested from top to bottom; the first true one runs and the rest are skipped:

```js
const speed = 70;
if (speed > 100) {
  console.log("Too fast!");
} else if (speed > 50) {
  console.log("Good speed.");
} else {
  console.log("Slow.");
}
```

A short way to pick one of two options and store it in a variable: `condition ? ifTrue : ifFalse`

```js
const lives = 0;
const message = lives > 0 ? "Keep going!" : "Game over";
```

When you compare one value with many options, `switch` looks tidy:

```js
switch (planet) {
  case "Mars":
    info = "The red planet";
    break;
  default:
    info = "Unknown";
}
```

If you forget `break`, the next option runs too!

Good to know: inside `if` you can use any value, not just `true`/`false`. `0`, `""` (empty text), `null`, `undefined` and `NaN` count as **false**; everything else counts as **true**.

examples.code:
const fuel = 15;
if (fuel < 20) {
  console.log("Warning: fuel is low!");
} else {
  console.log("Fuel is fine.");
}

const hour = 21;
const mode = hour >= 7 && hour < 19 ? "day" : "night";
console.log("Cockpit mode:", mode);

